you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  cyclone seroja hits australia after  causing devastation in indonesia  we're looking at if climate change is to  blame for more extreme weather  it's back to beer gardens in england we  look at why your locally sourced burger  is  three times greener than one in south  america  and green houses the italian architect  tackling building emissions  by 3d printing homes out of clay  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions this show aims to take you to  the heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come  now cyclone seroja has made landfall in  western australia causing damage to  homes and power supplies  such powerful cyclones are rare in  subtropical australia  the storm took an unusual path because  it interacted with another cyclone  odette  this phenomenon is known as the  fujihuara effect and it could become  more common  as climate change is expected to alter  weather patterns  before hitting the town of cal barrie in  australia the cyclone caused  far more damage when it struck indonesia  and east timor  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  prepare yourselves before we go into red  alert  [Music]  [Music]  so how likely is it that extreme and  unusual weather events are linked to  climate change  well joining me now is dr freddie otto  for the environmental change institute  at the university of  oxford dr freddie otto is it possible to  know if climate change  has had an impact on this cyclone  it is too early to say what the impact  of climate change  is on this cyclone but what we do know  is that because ocean temperatures are  now  higher in places where they they didn't  used to be  high enough for cyclones to form that we  do see an increase in cyclones in places  where they were very rare that doesn't  mean that every cyclone  is more likely or more extreme because  of climate change and we  don't see a general trend in that but we  do see them in places where people were  not used to cyclones  and extreme weather has always happened  so how do you attribute  a single event to climate change  extreme weather has always happened  indeed and every extreme weather event  has always  multiple causes but in the same way that  for example  after a volcanic eruption a big volcanic  eruption when you have lots of particles  in the high atmosphere that block  sunlight  you have an increased likelihood of cold  events  that come in the one or two years that  it takes for the particles  to wash out in the same way greenhouse  gases increase greenhouse gases in the  atmosphere  do change the intensity and frequency  of extreme weather events we see  particularly a strong  increase in intensity and frequency of  heat waves  we see fewer and less colder cold waves  and for all other types of extreme  events the changes are  very different from place to place in  the world so  in southern africa we see an increase in  droughts  in uh in northern europe we see an  increase in  extreme rainfall we also see an increase  actually  in extreme rainfall associated with  tropical cyclones  but because there are always multiple  effects playing together for most  complex weather events you really have  to look at the  at the local scale to know exactly what  role climate change is playing but we  are increasingly able to do that  and if global warming continues at its  current rate more generally what's going  to happen to the weather  so um if it's continuing at the current  rate  we will see even more devastating heat  waves people usually think about heat  waves oh it's  time to go to the beach but heat waves  are already even in the uk  the by far the most deadly extreme event  so last year alone  more than 250 uh 2500 people have been  killed  in in a heat wave and so globally we'll  see  much more of that we also see more  extreme rainfall events especially the  very localized events that have  a lot of rain in a very short time  leading to flash floods  and um for for other extremes it really  again  depends on where you are in the world so  droughts  also increase in the mediterranean and  parts of southern america  and uh when an uh  tropical cyclone is happening they are  more  devastating because of the increased  rainfall that climate change  brings with it dr freddie osso thank you  and you can hear more from dr freddie  otto on our climate cast podcast  and you can find that on apple spotify  or wherever you normally get your  podcast from  in today's other climate news now and a  new technology  could reduce carbon emissions from the  uk's aviation industry  by 60 million tonnes each year satavia  has developed software that re-routes  airplanes to prevent them from leaving  cloud trails  called contrails scientists believe  contrails are bad for the environment  because they trap heat  from the earth's atmosphere which warms  the air  john alumni and chris packham are  amongst the celebrities backing an  appeal  to save an ethiopian forest the charity  tree aid will give communities in matema  the tools and training to restore nearly  24 000 acres of woodland  the forest is particularly important as  frankincense is found there  which is used in essential oils around  the world  and ever wondered how your shopping  habits affect the environment  well mastercard is launching an online  calculator which lets you work out  the climate impact of what you buy the  calculator will also tell you the number  of  trees that are needed to offset your  purchase  now pop gardens and outdoor dining  reopen in england today and for many the  weeks of looking forward to a meal and a  pint at your local  are finally over so we thought we'd take  the opportunity to look at the climate  impact of a pub classic because  depending on  where you eat it seems not all burgers  are created equally  a cheeseburger chips and a pint of lager  is a pub classic so let's take a look at  the emissions produced if all the  ingredients are sourced  from the uk or the eu and that is a  total of  6.1 kilograms of carbon dioxide  equivalent now co2 equivalent means  the number of metric tons of co2  emissions with the same  global warming potential as one metric  ton  of another greenhouse gas now most of  that  6.1 kilos is the environmental impact of  beef and so let's compare that to a  similar meal  prepared in latin america and you get  three times  the emissions 19.8 kilograms  of co2 equivalent and again the majority  of that  is down to the beef but it's much higher  and that's because developing countries  have to transform  land to have space to graze cattle and  grow  crops meaning an increase in  deforestation and pollution  and worldwide three-quarters of  greenhouse gas emissions from the food  system  are from developing countries so let's  speak then to dr tara garnett from the  university of oxford about this  so dr tara garnett why does the meat  industry  produce emissions like this well there  are three main  sources of emissions from from the meat  industry um the first  is what you might call direct admission  so in the case of  beef that's methane which uh animals  burp out  and it is a very potent greenhouse gas  as you mentioned  the second source is land use change and  that can be either direct or indirect so  when it comes to direct we're talking  cattle grazing but there's also the  hidden  land use change impact um of feeding  animals  and that's pigs and poultry as well as  beef things like  soy and maize and so forth where forests  are cleared  and land is plowed up and that also  generates  carbon dioxide emissions and so that  links to the third point  it's it's the feed that they that they  eat that has an  impact on emissions and when you look at  this you find that  all meat and all animal products has  disproportionately high  environmental impacts well above  plant-based foods on average  and beef tends to rank um highest but it  does depend on  where you're sourcing your beef from um  when it comes to latin america  and brazil specifically um it is worth  remembering that  we live in an interconnected world  and those that beef is also being  produced for  export so a lot of the time we think  that our emissions don't look that high  but we're actually  um importing somebody else's problem or  rather we're exporting our problems to  to them and we don't want to put people  off their pub grub that they've been  looking forward to  for months but if they did want an  eco-friendly alternative what should  they do  um well i don't think there really is an  eco-friendly alternative  all beef generates and indeed all meat  generates high environmental impacts  i would say if you're going if you're  lucky enough to get into a pub today  enjoy your food enjoy your pint enjoy  your beef burger but  but remember that meat um that the best  way to reduce your climate impact  is actually to reduce just eat  less of it eat less of it and less often  and you know meat used to be a  celebration food is what you'd save up  for it was a sunday dinner  um and i think there's a role for meat  in our diets but  we need to we need to remember its value  and that means  um you know eating it for celebration  such as today  but shifting more to eating maybe a  veggie burger or other  plant-based foods dr tara garnett thank  you  and if you want to understand more about  what terms like co2 equivalent mean just  head to our website skynews.com  climate for our a to z glossary  now then let's take a quick look at our  data dashboard i want to show you  this number here which shows you how  much our planet is warming  right now because of human activity and  as you can see it's ticking  up every second now in the 2015 paris  agreement 196 countries around the world  agreed to work towards keeping global  warming below  2 degrees ideally at 1.5 degrees in  order to avoid the worst  impacts of climate change and below it  you can see  this clock here which is counting down  second by second  as we head towards that 1.5 degrees of  warming  now emissions from the building sector  reached their highest ever level in 2019  and manufacturing traditional materials  is particularly energy intensive cement  produces approximately eight percent of  the global total  of co2 a greater share than any country  other than china or the us  so italian architect mario cuccinella  wanted to do his bit  to try to come up with a solution his  answer  3d printing houses made entirely from  raw earth  buildings in the next 50 years will have  a very big impact on the environment the  paradox  of the next year will be in one side we  want to reduce emission  to zero the emission of co2 and on the  other side we need to build billions of  square meters  [Music]  tecla is the first experiment of a house  building in clay through the wasp  company so we we matching the idea of an  old material we will mix with the new  technology  3d printing and creating a house in clay  maybe the future is also to look our  path  and discovery the knowledge we are lost  in the last 250 years  maybe the next cities where we would  like to be  the smart city green city zero to a  mission city is a  city in clay but mixing digital  and old material  well that's it from us for now coming up  on the daily climate show  tomorrow the clever solution giving  single-use ppe  waste a new lease of life we've got  around  3 000 faulty single-use face masks here  what happens is they go inside the  machine  at the hospital for around an hour what  comes out is this 20 kilogram block of  plastic  that's at the same time tomorrow here on  sky news see you then  you

